.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_PEER_CERT_DER 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_der \- give the DER encoding of the verified client certificate
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const void *chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_der(const chttpsvr_req *req,
                                       size_t *len_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the DER encoding of the leaf certificate that the TLS client
of the request presented and that verified against the
ca_bundle_path of the server.  You get it only when
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) reports true for req.

With it you can read any field of the certificate with the library
you like, such as d2i_X509() of OpenSSL, or compare the whole
certificate with a pinned one.  chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_sha256(3) and
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject(3) give the two values that a handler
needs most often without a parse of its own.

The certificate is a property of the connection, so every request on
one keep-alive connection gets the same bytes, and a resumed TLS
session gets the certificate that the handshake which set up the
session verified.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
req      The request handle.
len_out  Optional.  Receives the length of the encoding in bytes, or
         0 whenever the function returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the DER bytes, valid for the lifetime of the request.
Do not free them.

NULL in every case where chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) reports
false (a plaintext connection, a server without a ca_bundle_path, a
client that presented no certificate, and a NULL req), and NULL as
well when an allocation fails.  Call chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3)
to tell these apart.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Like every other request accessor, call it only on the thread that
runs the handler or the middleware of req.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>
#include <openssl/x509.h>

static void handler(chttpsvr_req *req, chttpsvr_resp *resp, void *ctx)
{
    (void)ctx;
    size_t len = 0;
    const unsigned char *der = chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_der(req, &len);
    if (!der) {
        /* No verified certificate, or an allocation failed. */
        chttpsvr_resp_set_status(resp, chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(req)
                                           ? CHTTP_STATUS_INTERNAL_ERROR
                                           : CHTTP_STATUS_FORBIDDEN);
        return;
    }
    X509 *cert = d2i_X509(NULL, &der, (long)len);
    if (!cert) {
        chttpsvr_resp_set_status(resp, CHTTP_STATUS_INTERNAL_ERROR);
        return;
    }
    /* ... read the extensions that the policy needs ... */
    X509_free(cert);
    chttpsvr_resp_write_str(resp, "ok");
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_sha256 (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject (3),
